From: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
Subject: [PATCH] igs: arm the synchronous message wait before ringing the doorbell

nss_ifb_config_msg_tx_sync() sends the IFB config message and only then sets
the condition it is about to wait on. nss_if_tx_msg() rings the NSS doorbell,
so the firmware is already executing, and its ACK is processed by
nss_ifb_wake_up_cb() from the N2H softirq. An ACK that lands between the send
and the store finds an empty wait queue; the store then re-arms a condition
that nothing will ever clear again, and the caller sleeps out the full timeout.

That timeout is NSS_IFB_MSG_TIMEOUT, which is 600*HZ - ten minutes, not the one
minute its comment claims - and the sleep is uninterruptible. The only caller is
nss_mirred_init(), which sends two of these per redirect, and tc installs a u32
classifier under rtnl_lock (only cls_flower carries TCF_PROTO_OPS_DOIT_UNLOCKED),
so a single missed ACK stalls every netlink operation on the box for ten
minutes with no kernel output at all, and there are two chances per redirect.

Arm the condition before the send. Bring the timeout down to ten seconds to
match the sibling qdisc path: firmware turnaround is microseconds, and a
ten-minute uninterruptible hold of rtnl_lock is not a timeout, it is an outage.

This does not make the shared response slot safe against a late ACK crossing
into the next caller's wait: nss_ifb_wake_up_cb() carries no message identity,
so an ACK that arrives after its sender gave up clears whoever is waiting now
and stamps that caller with someone else's verdict. Clearing the condition on
the way out would not help - every caller re-arms it under the semaphore before
waiting, so the value left behind is never read. That hazard is older than this
change and needs a message identity to close; it is also gated behind an ACK
arriving more than ten seconds late, which has never been observed on hardware.

Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
---
--- a/nss_qdisc/igs/nss_ifb.h
+++ b/nss_qdisc/igs/nss_ifb.h
@@ -14,7 +14,7 @@
  **************************************************************************
  */
 
-#define NSS_IFB_MSG_TIMEOUT (600*HZ)	/* 1 min timeout */
+#define NSS_IFB_MSG_TIMEOUT (10*HZ)	/* firmware turnaround is microseconds */
 
 /*
  * nss_ifb_if_config
--- a/nss_qdisc/igs/nss_ifb.c
+++ b/nss_qdisc/igs/nss_ifb.c
@@ -286,15 +286,25 @@
 	}
 
 	down(&msg_response.sem);
+
+	/*
+	 * Arm the wait before ringing the doorbell, not after: nss_if_tx_msg()
+	 * hands the message to a firmware that answers in microseconds, and the
+	 * ACK callback runs from the N2H softirq. An ACK landing between the two
+	 * finds an empty wait queue, and the store below would then re-arm a
+	 * condition nothing will ever clear again - an uninterruptible sleep for
+	 * the whole timeout with rtnl_lock held, since this is only ever reached from
+	 * act_nssmirred's init under the classifier's RTNL section.
+	 */
+	msg_response.cond = 1;
 	ret = nss_if_tx_msg(nss_igs_get_context(), &nim);
 	if (ret != NSS_TX_SUCCESS) {
 		up(&msg_response.sem);
 		nss_igs_error("failed to send config message\n");
 		return -1;
 	}
 
-	msg_response.cond = 1;
 	if (!wait_event_timeout(msg_response.wq, msg_response.cond == 0, NSS_IFB_MSG_TIMEOUT)) {
 		nss_igs_error("config for attach interface msg timeout\n");
 		up(&msg_response.sem);
 		return -1;
